<!--
    A 64-bit PowerPC ELFv1 caller reloads its TOC with `ld r2,40(r1)` after a
    call that goes through a linker stub. The stub saved the TOC in that slot;
    the caller never writes it. A relative jump table after the call is
    addressed `addis/addi` from the reloaded r2, so with r2 unknown its base is
    unknown and the dispatch is a jump-as-call.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 abi-trust.
    P0 assertion:      option callmechanisms off|auto|on  (default auto).

    Synthetic bytes (hand-assembled), with the entry's TOC 0x3000 tracked:

        dispatch:  mflr r0; std r0,16(r1); stdu r1,-128(r1); addi r3,r1,112
                   bl stub; ld r2,40(r1); lwz r9,112(r1); cmplwi r9,3
                   bgt default; addis r10,r2,0; sldi r9,r9,2
                   addi r10,r10,-0x1fc0; lwax r9,r10,r9; add r9,r9,r10
                   mtctr r9; bctr
                   .long 0x10,0x18,0x20,0x28      (table at 0x1040)
                   cases li r3,11 / 22 / 33 / 44; default li r3,-1; epilogue
        stub:      std r2,40(r1); addi r2,r2,1; b second
        second:    lbz r3,0(r2); blr

    Pass 1 (option callmechanisms on) installs the PPC64 default model's
    `uponreturn` store of r2Save to 0x28(r1), so the reload reads back the
    TOC `bl` saved and the four-case switch is recovered. Pass 2 (option
    callmechanisms off) is the old model, and so is pass 3, the default
    `auto`: it acts only on an image whose ELFv1 function descriptors the
    loader validated, which a bare byte chunk does not have.
-->
